[7.420736, "o", "command -v grep || true; python3 --version; sed -n '1,120p' /app/docs/grep.txt"]
[7.420787, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[7.424216, "o", "Python 3.13.12\r\n"]
[7.426312, "o", "1 Introduction\r\n"]
[7.426357, "o", "**************\r\n"]
[7.426449, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.426513, "o", "Given one or more patterns, ‘grep’ searches input files for matches to\r\nthe patterns.  When it finds a match in a line, it copies the line to\r\n"]
[7.426551, "o", "standard output (by default), or produces whatever other sort of output\r\nyou have requested with options.\r\n\r\n   Though ‘grep’ expects to do the matching on text, it has no limits on\r\ninput line length other than available memory, and it can match\r\narbitrary characters within a line.  If the final byte of an input file\r\nis not a newline, ‘grep’ silently supplies one.  Since newline is also a\r\nseparator for the list of patterns, there is no way to match newline\r\ncharacters in a text.\r\n\r\n"]
[7.426775, "o", "2 Invoking ‘grep’\r\n*****************\r\n\r\nThe general synopsis of the ‘grep’ command line is\r\n\r\n     grep [OPTION...] [PATTERNS] [FILE...]\r\n\r\n"]
[7.426842, "o", "There can be zero or more OPTION arguments, and zero or more FILE\r\narguments.  The PATTERNS argument contains one or more patterns\r\nseparated by newlines, and is omitted when patterns are given via the\r\n‘-e PATTERNS’ or ‘-f FILE’ options.  Typically PATTERNS should be quoted\r\nwhen ‘grep’ is used in a shell command.\r\n\r\n* Menu:\r\n\r\n* Command-line Options::        Short and long names, grouped by category.\r\n* Environment Variables::       POSIX, GNU generic, and GNU grep specific.\r\n* Exit Status::                 Exit status returned by ‘grep’.\r\n* grep Programs::               ‘grep’ programs.\r\n\r\n2.1 Command-line Options\r\n========================\r\n\r\n‘grep’ comes with a rich set of options: some from POSIX and some being\r\nGNU extensions.  Long option names are always a GNU extension, even for\r\noptions that are from POSIX specifications.  Options that are specified\r\nby POSIX, under their short names, are explicitly marked as such to\r\nfacilitate POSIX-portable programming.  A few option names"]
[7.426877, "o", " are provided\r\nfor compatibility with older or more exotic implementations.\r\n\r\n* Menu:\r\n\r\n* Generic Program Information::\r\n* Matching Control::\r\n* General Output Control::\r\n* Output Line Prefix Control::\r\n* Context Line Control::\r\n* File and Directory Selection::\r\n* Other Options::\r\n\r\n   Several additional options control which variant of the ‘grep’\r\nmatching engine is used.  *Note grep Programs::.\r\n\r\n2.1.1 Generic Program Information\r\n---------------------------------\r\n\r\n‘--help’\r\n     Print a usage message briefly summarizing the command-line options\r\n     and the bug-reporting address, then exit.\r\n\r\n‘-V’\r\n‘--version’\r\n     Print the version number of ‘grep’ to the standard output stream.\r\n     This version number should be included in all bug reports.\r\n\r\n2.1.2 Matching Control\r\n----------------------\r\n\r\n‘-e PATTERNS’\r\n"]
[7.426936, "o", "‘--regexp=PATTERNS’\r\n     Use PATTERNS as one or more patterns; newlines within PATTERNS\r\n     separate each pattern from the next.  If this option is used\r\n     multiple times or is combined with the ‘-f’ (‘--file’) option,\r\n"]
[7.426984, "o", "     search for all patterns given.  Typically PATTERNS should be quoted\r\n     when ‘grep’ is used in a shell command.  (‘-e’ is specified by\r\n     POSIX.)\r\n\r\n"]
[7.427047, "o", "‘-f FILE’\r\n‘--file=FILE’\r\n     Obtain patterns from FILE, one per line.  If this option is used\r\n     multiple times or is combined with the ‘-e’ (‘--regexp’) option,\r\n     search for all patterns given.  The empty file contains zero\r\n     patterns, and therefore matches nothing.  (‘-f’ is specified by\r\n     POSIX.)\r\n\r\n‘-i’\r\n"]
[7.427089, "o", "‘-y’\r\n‘--ignore-case’\r\n     Ignore case distinctions in patterns and input data, so that\r\n     characters that differ only in case match each other.  Although\r\n     this is straightforward when letters differ in case only via\r\n     lowercase-uppercase pairs, the behavior is unspecified in other\r\n     situations.  For example, uppercase “S” has an unusual lowercase\r\n     counterpart “ſ” (Unicode character U+017F, LATIN SMALL LETTER LONG\r\n     S) in many locales, and it is unspecified whether this unusual\r\n     character matches “S” or “s” even though uppercasing it yields “S”.\r\n     Another example: the lowercase German letter “ß” (U+00DF, LATIN\r\n"]
[7.427173, "o", "     SMALL LETTER SHARP S) is normally capitalized as the two-character\r\n     string “SS” but it does not match “SS”, and it might not match the\r\n     uppercase letter “ẞ” (U+1E9E, LATIN CAPITAL LETTER SHARP S) even\r\n"]
[7.427773, "o", "     though lowercasing the latter yields the former.\r\n\r\n     ‘-y’ is an obsolete synonym that is provided for compatibility.\r\n     (‘-i’ is specified by POSIX.)\r\n\r\n‘--no-ignore-case’\r\n     Do not ignore case distinctions in patterns and input data.  This\r\n     is the default.  This option is useful for passing to shell scripts\r\n     that already use ‘-i’, in order to cancel its effects because the\r\n     two options override each other.\r\n\r\n"]
[7.427819, "o", "‘-v’\r\n‘--invert-match’\r\n     Invert the sense of matching, to select non-matching lines.  (‘-v’\r\n     is specified by POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.434342, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@35328358d973:/app# "]
[14.907795, "i", "grep(){ python3 - \"$@\" <<'PY'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1].lower()\nfor i,l in enumerate(open('/app/docs/grep.txt',errors='replace')):\n if p in l.lower(): print(f'{i+1}:{l}',end='')\nPY\n}; grep 'regular expression' | head -40; grep 'only-matching'; grep 'byte-offset'; grep 'binary-files'; grep 'group-separator'\n"]
[14.908663, "o", "grep(){ python3 - \"$@\" <<'PY'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[14.910352, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> import sys\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> p=sys.argv[1].lower()\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> for i,l in enumerate(open('/app/docs/grep.txt',errors='replace')):\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>  if p in l.lower(): print(f'{i+1}:{l}',end='')\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> PY\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> }; grep 'regular expression' | head -40; grep 'only-matching'; grep 'byte-offset'; grep 'binary-files'; grep 'group-separator'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[14.983409, "o", "134:     regular expression with ‘\\<’ and ‘\\>’.  For example, although ‘grep\r\n142:     regular expression patterns, this is like parenthesizing each\r\n723:     Interpret patterns as basic regular expressions (BREs).  This is\r\n728:     Interpret patterns as extended regular expressions (EREs).  (‘-E’\r\n733:     Interpret patterns as fixed strings, not regular expressions.\r\n738:     Interpret patterns as Perl-compatible regular expressions (PCREs).\r\n744:3 Regular Expressions\r\n747:A “regular expression” is a pattern that describes a set of strings.\r\n748:Regular expressions are constructed analogously to arithmetic\r\n750:‘grep’ understands three different versions of regular expression\r\n755:extended regular expressions; differences for basic regular expressions\r\n756:are summarized afterwards.  Perl-compatible regular expressions give\r\n776:In regular expressions, the characters ‘.?*+{|()[\\^$’ are “special\r\n784:   A regular expression may be followed by one of several repetition\r\n811:   The"]
[14.983484, "o", " empty regular expression matches the empty string.  Two regular\r\n812:expressions may be concatenated; the resulting regular expression\r\n816:   Two regular expressions may be joined by the infix operator ‘|’.  The\r\n817:resulting regular expression matches any string matching either of the\r\n825:   Not every character string is a valid regular expression.  *Note\r\n835:example, the regular expression ‘[0123456789]’ matches any single digit,\r\n993:of regular expressions is extended in future versions.\r\n1008:the regular expression.  For example, ‘(a)\\1’ matches ‘aa’.  If the\r\n1014:multiple regular expressions are given with ‘-e’ or from a file (‘-f\r\n1019:3.6 Basic vs Extended Regular Expressions\r\n1022:Basic regular expressions differ from extended regular expressions in\r\n1044:3.7 Problematic Regular Expressions\r\n1047:Some strings are “invalid regular expressions” and cause ‘grep’ to issue\r\n1051:   Also, some regular expressions have “unspecified behavior” and should\r\n1060:   The fol"]
[14.983511, "o", "lowing regular expression constructs are invalid on all\r\n1064:   • A basic regular expression containing a back-reference ‘\\N’\r\n1076:   • Unescaped ‘\\’ at the end of a regular expression.\r\n1080:   • A ‘\\{’ in a basic regular expression that does not start an\r\n1083:   • A basic regular expression with unbalanced ‘\\(’ or ‘\\)’, or an\r\n1084:     extended regular expression with unbalanced ‘(’.\r\n1107:   • A basic regular expression that uses ‘\\?’, ‘\\+’, or ‘\\|’.\r\n1109:   • An extended regular expression that uses back-references.\r\n1111:   • An empty regular expression, subexpression, or alternative.  For\r\n1115:   • In a basic regular expression, an anchoring ‘^’ that appears\r\n1119:   • In a basic regular expression, a repetition operator that directly\r\n1122:   • In an extended regular expression, unescaped ‘{’ that does not\r\n"]
[15.002111, "o", "220:‘--only-matching’\r\n251:     line of output.  If ‘-o’ (‘--only-matching’) is specified, print\r\n303:line more than once.  If the ‘-o’ (‘--only-matching’) option is\r\n"]
[15.020196, "o", "249:‘--byte-offset’\r\n"]
[15.02039, "o", "491:     When using text I/O ‘--byte-offset’ (‘-b’) counts and\r\n"]
[15.029344, "o", "356:     ‘--binary-files=text’ option.\r\n358:‘--binary-files=TYPE’\r\n388:     _Warning:_ The ‘-a’ (‘--binary-files=text’) option might output\r\n441:     is equivalent to the ‘--binary-files=without-match’ option.\r\n492:     ‘--binary-files’ heuristics apply to input data after text-I/O\r\n493:     processing.  Also, the ‘--binary-files’ heuristics need not agree\r\n"]
[15.029445, "o", "1172:character.  However, unless the ‘-a’ (‘--binary-files=text’) option is\r\n"]
[15.031563, "o", "1348:     ‘--binary-files=text’ option.  To eliminate the “Binary file\r\n1349:     matches” messages, use the ‘-I’ or ‘--binary-files=without-match’\r\n"]
